@* Landing — the front door, and the only screen with no content of its own.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Two audiences at once: someone evaluating the work, and the journalist the
   app is built for. It serves the first without pretending not to be a
   demonstration, which is why the disclosure sits in the second paragraph
   rather than in a footer.

   NOT A MARKETING PAGE. No feature grid, no testimonials, no invented
   statistics, no logos. This is the easiest screen in the app to over-design,
   precisely because it has no real content to defer to — so it states what the
   app does, that it is simulated, and how to proceed, and then stops.

   HEADING, LEDE, ONE WAY ON. This screen used to carry two weighted entry
   points and a three-point list of what a signed-in record shows. Both are
   gone. The two-entry section was the front door doing the navigation's job,
   and it stopped being true the moment sign-in became a persistent affordance
   in the session row above every screen; the three-point list described the
   record to people who had not asked to see it yet, on the one screen with no
   room to be wrong about what a visitor came for. What is left is what the
   register asks for: what this is, that it is a demonstration, and one way on.

   The demo credentials are deliberately NOT here. They belong beside the form
   they are typed into, one click away. A reviewer who lands on a credential
   dump learns what to type before learning what they are looking at, and
   publishing them twice would create two places to keep in sync. *@

@page "/"
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject SimulatedSessionProvider Session
@inject LocaleService L

<PageTitle>@L[Locale, "app.name"]</PageTitle>

@if (justSignedOut)
{
    @* Signing out says so. Leaving a person to infer it from a session row
       that quietly stopped naming them is the same class of silence this app
       exists to replace, at a smaller scale. *@
    <p class="landing__signed-out" role="status">
        <strong>@L[Locale, "landing.signedOutStrong"]</strong>
        @L[Locale, "landing.signedOutBody"]
    </p>
}

<h1>@L[Locale, "landing.title"]</h1>

<p class="landing__lede">@L[Locale, "landing.lede"]</p>

<p class="landing__disclosure" role="note">
    <strong>@L[Locale, "landing.disclosureStrong"]</strong>
    @L[Locale, "landing.disclosureBody"]
</p>

@* One way on, and it points at the matches rather than at the form. Sign-in
   has a persistent door of its own now — the session row carries it on every
   screen — so the front door does not need to be a second one, and a browse-
   first CTA is the honest one for a visitor who has not yet been told what
   there is to sign in for. *@
<a class="btn btn-primary landing__cta" href="matches">@L[Locale, "landing.cta"]</a>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public AppLocale Locale { get; set; }

    private bool justSignedOut;

    /// <summary>
    /// A signed-in visitor has no use for the front door. Redirecting rather
    /// than rendering a second entry point keeps one answer to "where is my
    /// record", which is the whole reason the record got a named route.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        if (Session.IsSignedIn)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo("record", replace: true);
            return;
        }

        // Asked once and cleared by the asking, so a refresh does not repeat an
        // announcement about something that happened a while ago.
        justSignedOut = Session.ConsumeSignOutAnnouncement();
    }
}
